Some explanations on the format of the renumbering table.


1. Format of the file
2. Given an ideal (p,r) on side s, how do I find its index in the table ?
3. Given an index in the table (and the polynomial pair), how do I find the
   corresponding ideal ?


0. Convention
=============

A rational ideal above p is represented by (p,r) where r does not matter
(renumber_t::p_r_from_index returns r=0).
A projective ideal above p is represented by (p,p).

1. Format of the file
=====================

The format of the file is described in filter/README.fileformat

Two formats exist. Both begin with the same text header, whose first
non-comment line is the format number.

 - 20220411 ("flat"): the table follows the header as text, one "p vr"
   pair per line, in decimal.

 - 20260919 ("binary", the default since then): the header is the one
   above, followed by one last line giving the offset of the table in
   the file, the size in bytes of one of the two values of an entry (4
   or 8, depending on SIZEOF_P_R_VALUES on the machine that wrote the
   file), and the byte order ('L' or 'B'). That line is padded with
   spaces so that it ends exactly where the table begins, which is a
   multiple of 4096; a reader that cannot seek gets to the table by
   reading that line to its end. The table itself follows as pairs of
   integers in machine representation.

   A file in this format that is not compressed, and whose entries
   have the width this binary was compiled for, is mmapped rather than
   read. Otherwise it is read and converted.

   Files written between 2026-09-19 and 2026-10-17 carry the number
   20250919 instead. It is accepted as a synonym.


2. Given an ideal (p,r) on side s, how do I find its index in the table ?
=========================================================================

* Compute vr
    vr = (nsides - 1)*(p + 1) if s is the rational side
    vr = s'*(p + 1) + r otherwise, where s' is the number of non-rational
         sides below s (with one rational side and two sides, vr = r)

* Find the entry j of the table such that table[j] = (p, vr). All entries
  for a given p are contiguous, and p is non-decreasing in the table.

* The index of the ideal of (p,r) on side s is j + above_bad, where
  above_bad is the number of additional columns plus the total number of
  bad ideals (it is also given in the header, by the comment line
  "# renumber table for all indices above ...").
  Bad ideals and additional columns have indices below above_bad.

This is renumber_t::index_from_p_r in utils/renumber.cpp.


3. Given an index in the table (and the polynomial pair), how do I find the
   corresponding ideal ?
===========================================================================

* Let (p, vr) = table[i - above_bad]

* Let r = vr. For each non-rational side s, in increasing order:
    if r <= p, the ideal is (p,r) on side s ; stop.
    otherwise, r = r - (p + 1)

* If r = 0 at the end, the ideal is the one above p on the rational side.

This is renumber_t::p_r_from_index in utils/renumber.cpp.
